**************************************************************************
*     At each time step FEQ prints what is called an iteration log
*     giving some statistics for each iteration for the current time
*     step.  Look in the output file at simulation time 3.5 hours.
*     After giving the date and time and the time step in seconds,
*     FEQ prints the iteration log.  The iteration number appears
*     in the column labeled ITER.  The maximum relative correction(or
*     loosely speaking, the relative error) at each iteration appears
*     under the column headed RERROR.  The branch number of the
*     node at which the maximum relative correction appeared is
*     given next under the column headed BRA.  The node on the
*     branch is given in the next column.  If the branch number is
*     zero, an invalid branch number for input, it means that the
*     node number is an exterior node number.  If the node number is
*     positive it shows that the maximum correction occurred for flow;
*     if it is negative it shows that the maximum correction occurred
*     in the depth value.  The next column gives the maximum rescaled
*     residual in the equation system.  The equation system is rescaled
*     so that the maximum coefficient in the Jacobian matrix used in
*     Newton's method not greater than 1.0 in absolute value.    The
*     LOC column gives the location of the maximum residual in the
*     equation system.  A negative location denotes an equation number
*     in the exterior matrix and a positive number an equation
*     number in the interior matrix.   The next column, headed by
*     SUMSQR, gives the sum of the squares of the rescaled residuals for
*     both the interior and exterior system residuals.  The final column
*     shows the number of variables which are currently outside the
*     convergence criteria.
*
*     The line following the last iteration in the iteration log gives
*     additional information about the computations.  CSUR gives the
*     relative correction in the surcharge storage which might be present
*     if storm sewers are being simulated.    The AT ND stands for at node
*     and the branch and node at which the surcharge storage with the
*     maximum relative correction exists is given.  The branch is given
*     first followed by a colon and then the node number on the branch.
*     RLTQ gives the maximum ratio of lateral inflow to the flow in an
*     element.  Large ratios( > 1) can relate to computational problems
*     associated with convergence problems.  The location of the element
*     follows in branch:node-on-branch sequence.    The last entry on the
*     line is the simulation time per iteration so far.
*
*     The results are given next with the elapsed time in seconds from
*     the start of the run and the modified julian time in days.
*     The columns have the following meanings:
*
*     NODE - node number on the branch
*     NODEID - node identification you provided
*     STAT - the station of the node. Precision varies with SFAC.
*     FLOW - flow rate in modified fortran floating point output.
*            To save space only a one digit exponent is used and the
*            E is deleted.  Thus a sign for the exponent on the base 10
*            always appears.  It is unlikely that flows greater than
*            one billion cfs will be needed.
*     AREA - the flow area
*     VEL - the average velocity at the node
*     DEPTH - the maximum water depth at the node
*     ELEV - the water surface elevation
*     TOP - the top width at the current elevation
*     FR - the square of the Froude number at the node.  Critical flow is
*          taken from tables of type 12 if they are used.  Otherwise the
*          the critical flow is computed ignoring any effects of the
*          velocity distribution.  Thus the Froude number is crude if
*          overbank flow is present and tables of type 1 are used.
*     LAT IN - lateral inflow coming from the land surface.  Only
*              non-zero when DIFFUS=YES is used.
*
*     The ponding volume in storm sewer surcharge storage is given for
*     each branch and for the network as a whole.
*
*     Each exterior node's values are also output.  QE1 denotes the flow
*     at the node, YE1 denotes the depth,  ELEV denotes the elevation of the
*     water surface, and the two STORAGE columns give the storage volume
*     present in any level-pool reservoirs in both cubic feet and
*     acre-feet.
*
*     The final line of output for a complete output at a time step gives
*     a mass balance check.  S1 is the initial volume in the stream system,
*     including channel, reservoirs, and storm sewer surcharge, S2 is the
*     volume in the stream system at the end of the current time step.
*     I is the volume of inflow from the start of the simulation; and
*     Q is the volume of outflow for the same period.  D gives the
*     the water balance discrepancy in cubic feet; and R gives the
*     discrepancy relative to S1 + I.  If the balance errors were zero
*     then S1 + I = S2 + Q.
*
*     FEQ issues a message each time it changes the time step.  If the
*     time step is already at its maximum value, message is issued even
*     though the time step is not changed because FEQ tries to change the
*     time step.
*
*     At the end of the simulation period, FEQ gives a summary of the
*     maximum flows, and elevations reached.  The minimum flow is also given
*     because one or more branches or nodes may have had negative flow values
*     which were larger in absolute value than the positive flow values.
*
*     On some systems a summary of the computation time will be given.
*
***************************************************************************
